I am biased since I am a local, but Utah Valley is on the cusp of recognition as a tech hotbed. Just in our office park in the corner of Provo, we have<p><pre><code>  1. http:&#x2F;&#x2F;www.ancestry.com&#x2F;
  2. http:&#x2F;&#x2F;www.qualtrics.com&#x2F;
  3. us, http:&#x2F;&#x2F;i.tv
  4. http:&#x2F;&#x2F;www.moneydesktop.com&#x2F;
  5. http:&#x2F;&#x2F;www.vivint.com&#x2F;en&#x2F;, recently bought for $2 billion[0]
</code></pre>
There are also a bunch of other smaller tech companies.<p><a href="http://www.domo.com/" rel="nofollow">http:&#x2F;&#x2F;www.domo.com&#x2F;</a> and the Adobe campus are also about 10 minutes away, and there are various tech companies scattered all the way up through Salt Lake.<p><a href="http://www.ng-conf.org/" rel="nofollow">http:&#x2F;&#x2F;www.ng-conf.org&#x2F;</a> is coming to SLC next year, and the local tech meetups are active and widely attended. Combine the good tech scene with the extremely low cost of living and it is a great place to be.<p><pre><code>  0: http:&#x2F;&#x2F;seekingalpha.com&#x2F;article&#x2F;878211-blackstone-buys-vivint-what-you-need-to-know</code></pre>
